(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-supraventricular-arrhythmias-quiz"]={"title":"Arrhythmias I: Supraventricular Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A junctional rhythm due to sick sinus syndrome or high-grade atrioventricular block is treated with what?","choices":["Adenosine","Catheter ablation","Permanent pacemaker","Observation alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. If a junctional rhythm is due to complete or high-grade atrioventricular block or to sick sinus syndrome, a permanent pacemaker is placed. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Why is adenosine useful when focal atrial tachycardia is suspected?","choices":["It reliably ends the focus","It is the long-term cure","It prevents recurrences","Its failure is diagnostic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Adenosine is an important diagnostic tool because it is usually less effective at terminating focal atrial tachycardia than the reentry tachycardias that depend on the atrioventricular node. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which is a listed risk factor for atrial fibrillation?","choices":["Sleep apnea","Hypothyroidism","Low body weight","Young age"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Atrial fibrillation is commonly seen with hypertension, diabetes, cardiac disease, family history of atrial fibrillation, obesity, sleep apnea, excessive alcohol and older age. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What is the first-line treatment for stable supraventricular tachycardia?","choices":["Adenosine","Synchronized cardioversion","Intravenous procainamide","A vagal maneuver"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Stable supraventricular tachycardia is treated first with a vagal maneuver, then adenosine (which may be repeated once); an unstable patient gets immediate synchronized cardioversion. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A slow sinus rhythm that is causing hypotension and confusion is treated first with which drug?","choices":["Metoprolol","Digoxin","Atropine","Adenosine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Acute, symptomatic, hemodynamically unstable bradycardia is treated first with atropine, then a temporary pacemaker if it fails; chronic symptomatic disease needs a permanent pacemaker. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"In tachy-brady syndrome, which tachyarrhythmia is most common?","choices":["Atrial flutter","Ventricular tachycardia","Sinus tachycardia","Atrial fibrillation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In tachy-brady syndrome a tachyarrhythmia, most commonly atrial fibrillation, alternates with symptomatic bradycardia or with offset pauses. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What is the usual rate of atrial fibrillation?","choices":["40 to 60","60 to 100","110 to 160","250 to 300"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Atrial fibrillation usually runs at 110 to 160 beats per minute, although the rate can fall below 80 or rise above 200; the rhythm is irregularly irregular with absent P waves and a narrow QRS. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"In whom are the symptoms of supraventricular tachycardia more pronounced?","choices":["Elderly patients or heart disease","Young, healthy athletes","Those with paroxysmal episodes","Women more than men"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Symptoms of supraventricular tachycardia, such as palpitations, dyspnea and chest discomfort, may be more pronounced in the elderly and in those with underlying structural heart disease. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which physical finding fits atrioventricular nodal reentrant tachycardia?","choices":["Absent leg pulses","Neck pulsations","Kussmaul sign","Pulsus paradoxus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Patients typically complain of sudden-onset palpitations and neck pulsations, seen on examination as the frog sign. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"What defines long-standing persistent atrial fibrillation?","choices":["Continuous for 7 days to a year","Episodes lasting under a week","Agreeing to stop rhythm control","Continuous for over a year"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Long-standing persistent atrial fibrillation is continuous for more than one year; persistent is more than 7 days but less than a year, and permanent is defined by a joint decision, not by duration. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Atrioventricular nodal reentrant tachycardia is more common in whom?","choices":["Men","People with lung disease","People with heart surgery","Women"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Atrioventricular nodal reentrant tachycardia affects women far more than men, commonly without heart disease, spontaneously or with exertion, caffeine or alcohol. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"How is a junctional rhythm generally tolerated?","choices":["Well","Poorly, with syncope","It degenerates to fibrillation","Usually needs a pacemaker"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A junctional rhythm is generally well tolerated; management depends on the cause, from no treatment in a healthy person to a pacemaker for block or sick sinus syndrome. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Which is a listed cause of junctional rhythm?","choices":["Anemia","Digoxin toxicity","Hyperthyroidism","Heavy caffeine use"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Causes of junctional rhythm include sinus node dysfunction or marked bradycardia, inferior myocardial infarction, the acute period after cardiac surgery and digoxin toxicity. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"For stable, symptomatic focal atrial tachycardia, which acute drugs are used?","choices":["Intravenous atropine","Intravenous magnesium","Intravenous procainamide","Intravenous rate control"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hemodynamically stable, symptomatic focal atrial tachycardia is treated with an intravenous beta blocker or nondihydropyridine calcium channel blocker, while the precipitating cause is found and treated. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Besides drugs and thyroid disease, which condition is a listed cause of sinus bradycardia?","choices":["Pain or anxiety","Hypoxia","Intracranial hypertension","Pulmonary embolism"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Increased intracranial pressure is a listed cause of sinus bradycardia, together with sinus node ischemia, late-stage infection, hypothyroidism and the rate-slowing drugs. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which P wave finding fits atrioventricular reentrant tachycardia?","choices":["Hidden or retrograde P waves","Sawtooth flutter waves","Three or more P-wave shapes","One upright P before each QRS"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In atrioventricular reentrant tachycardia the P waves are hidden or retrograde, and the PR interval is hidden or short, in a regular rhythm usually at 200 to 300. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which condition is a pathologic cause of sinus bradycardia?","choices":["Hypovolemia","Hypothyroidism","Hyperthyroidism","Anemia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hypothyroidism is a listed cause of sinus bradycardia, with sinus node ischemia, increased intracranial pressure, late-stage infection and drugs such as beta blockers and digoxin. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Persistent, symptomatic premature atrial contractions are treated first with which?","choices":["Amiodarone","A beta blocker","Adenosine","Procainamide"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Persistent, symptomatic premature atrial contractions are treated with a beta blocker such as metoprolol if not contraindicated, or ablation when they are frequent or trigger atrial fibrillation. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"In sinus arrhythmia, how does the heart rate change with breathing?","choices":["Unchanged; the P-wave shape varies","Faster on inspiration, slower on expiration","Slower on inspiration, faster on expiration","Faster in both phases of the breath"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Sinus arrhythmia is irregular with a pattern: the sinus rate speeds up with inspiration and slows with expiration, while every beat keeps a normal P wave and narrow QRS. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"How is hemodynamically stable atrial flutter treated first?","choices":["Synchronized cardioversion","Catheter ablation","Repeated adenosine","Beta or calcium channel blocker"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Stable atrial flutter is rate-controlled with a beta blocker or calcium channel blocker; an unstable patient gets synchronized cardioversion. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which drug is a direct oral anticoagulant preferred in nonvalvular atrial fibrillation?","choices":["Procainamide","Apixaban","Warfarin","Aspirin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Apixaban is one of the direct oral anticoagulants preferred in nonvalvular atrial fibrillation, with dabigatran, edoxaban and rivaroxaban. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Adenosine slows a tachycardia and reveals atrial fibrillation with Wolff-Parkinson-White. Which drug is used?","choices":["Intravenous atropine","Intravenous magnesium","Intravenous procainamide","A second dose of adenosine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. When slowing from adenosine reveals atrial fibrillation with Wolff-Parkinson-White, intravenous procainamide is used and adenosine is not repeated; adenosine and the other nodal blockers leave the accessory pathway unopposed. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What defines inappropriate sinus tachycardia?","choices":["Tachycardia caused by fever","Tachycardia during exercise","Tachycardia from a stimulant","Unexplained once causes excluded"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Inappropriate sinus tachycardia is an elevated heart rate that remains unexplained after secondary causes have been excluded, so it is a diagnosis of exclusion. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What is the tachy-brady form of sick sinus syndrome?","choices":["Slowing on expiration, speeding up on inspiration","Fast atrial rhythms alternating with slow ones","An impulse that cannot exit the node","An impulse the node fails to generate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In tachy-brady syndrome a tachyarrhythmia, most commonly atrial fibrillation, alternates with symptomatic bradycardia or offset pauses; fast atrial rhythms alternate with slow rhythms or pauses. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which medication class is a recognized cause of sinus bradycardia?","choices":["Stimulants","Beta-agonist inhalers","Beta blockers","Anticholinergics"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers, nondihydropyridine calcium channel blockers and digoxin are the medication causes of sinus bradycardia, alongside hypothyroidism, sinus node ischemia and raised intracranial pressure. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What is the usual rate of a junctional rhythm?","choices":["40 to 60","60 to 100","100 to 160","250 to 300"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A junctional rhythm is regular, usually at 40 to 60 beats per minute, with a narrow QRS and P waves that are inverted, buried or after the QRS. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Which counseling fits benign premature atrial contractions?","choices":["Begin anticoagulation","Take a lifelong beta blocker","Avoid triggers; reassure","Stop exercising"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Counsel the patient to avoid potential precipitants such as excessive alcohol, stress and caffeine, and give reassurance of the benign nature of the rhythm. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Which therapy is usually curative for focal atrial tachycardia?","choices":["A permanent pacemaker","Atropine","Catheter ablation","Repeated adenosine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Catheter ablation of the ectopic focus is usually curative for focal atrial tachycardia, in about 90%; oral beta blockers or calcium channel blockers are the initial chronic therapy. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which rhythm describes atrial fibrillation?","choices":["Irregularly irregular, no P waves","Regular, with sawtooth waves","Irregular, three P-wave shapes","Regular, with hidden P waves"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Atrial fibrillation is irregularly irregular with absent P waves and no PR interval, usually at 110 to 160 with a narrow QRS. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which type of atrioventricular reentrant tachycardia is more common?","choices":["Orthodromic","Antidromic","Multifocal","Slow-fast"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Atrioventricular reentrant tachycardia is orthodromic or antidromic, and orthodromic is more common; it produces a regular, narrow complex tachycardia. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 33"}]};
